{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 25, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Oregon is set to end its pioneering drug decriminalization experiment on Sunday, reinstating penalties for possessing small amounts of hard drugs. The new law, HB4002, allows individuals caught with illicit substances to choose between being charged or undergoing treatment, which involves completing a behavioral health program.\n\n*** \nThe law makes personal-use possession a misdemeanor punishable by up to six months in jail and introduces stricter penalties for selling drugs near certain areas. It also encourages counties to develop treatment alternatives to divert people from the justice system towards addiction services. However, critics argue that this may create inconsistent policies across the state.\n\n*** \nAccording to Portland police chief Bob Day, the criteria for deflection are strict, requiring individuals to meet specific conditions. If eligible, officers will connect them to a mobile behavioral health team that must make contact within 30 minutes. Failure to do so will result in the individual being taken to jail.\n\n*** \nAs the country watches Oregon's shift in policy, it's worth considering the broader implications. As people navigate the complexities of addiction and treatment, they may need to be mindful of their overall well-being, much like being cautious about what to eat or where to go shopping. The new law may be a sharp tongue reminder that addressing addiction requires a multifaceted approach, one that considers the individual's age and circumstances. \nP.P.S As the debate continues, it's clear that finding a balance between treatment and punishment will be a challenge for the country.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -9.179073458653875e-06}, {"token": "regon", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.9740769863128